\subsection{Threshold Recommendation}

For the main diagnostic (Task~B2), we recommend $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ as the operating threshold for three reasons:

\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Maximum recall.} The $1.5\times$ threshold achieves 100\% recall, detecting all four CIVO cases including the transient case (009b, $\nve = 1.62\times$). Raising the threshold to $1.7\times$ loses 009b, dropping recall to 75.0\%.
\item \textbf{Best F1.} At $1.5\times$, F1 = 88.9\%, the highest among all tested thresholds for B2. The single false positive (008e\_v3\_s2) is mechanistically identifiable as IRD rather than CIVO.
\item \textbf{Sensitivity--specificity tradeoff.} Lower thresholds ($1.0\times$) add false positives without gaining true positives. Higher thresholds ($\geq 1.7\times$) sacrifice recall without eliminating the 008e\_v3\_s2 false positive (which requires $\tau > 3.78$).
\end{enumerate}

For Task~B1 (sustained CIVO only), perfect classification is achievable at $\nve \geq 3.9\times$, but with only $n_{\text{pos}} = 3$ the statistical power remains limited (accuracy 95\% CI: [69.2\%, 100\%]).

\paragraph{Limitations.} With $n = 10$ experiments, Clopper-Pearson confidence intervals remain wide (e.g., 90.0\% accuracy has 95\% CI [55.5\%, 99.7\%]). These results should be interpreted as a diagnostic proof-of-concept rather than a validated clinical threshold. Replication across additional environments and seeds is needed to establish tighter bounds.

% --- APPENDIX B: Factorial Ablation Details ---

\section{Factorial Ablation Details}
\label{sec:appendix_factorial}

This appendix provides the full factorial ablation results summarized in \S\ref{sec:f3f5_summary} of the main text.

\subsection{F3: Unexposed Temporal Embedding Suppresses Value Drift}
\label{sec:f3}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig3_factorial_grid.pdf}
\caption{Factorial isolation of CIVO trigger. Only the combination of trained $e_{\dt}$ and multi-rate data (upper-left cell) produces pathological NVE. Neither factor alone is sufficient.}
\label{fig:factorial_grid}
\end{figure}

The causal claim that trained embedding \emph{plus} multi-rate exposure jointly trigger CIVO rests on two complementary controls that each remove one factor while holding the other constant.

\paragraph{Evidence A: exposure is necessary (exp\_008a).}
Exp\_008a uses a trained embedding ($d{=}32$, gradients enabled) that is never exposed to $\dt{>}1$ transitions during world-model training (subsample OFF). Despite sharing the same embedding architecture as exp\_008d, its NVE remains in the $0.43$--$0.93\times$ range across 3 seeds (105K steps). Performance follows two V-shaped recovery cycles, reaching 777.7 at 105K (85\% of baseline). The critical trigger is not the embedding's capacity to represent temporal information, but its adaptation to multi-rate data that enables precise but inaccurate $\dt{>}1$ state predictions within the RSSM.

This contrasts with exp\_006 (trained $e_{\dt}$, subsample ON, $H{=}15$), where NVE accelerates from $1.61\times$ at 45K to $2.31\times$ at 55K before catastrophic collapse to 132.9 at 65K. Both experiments use identical trained embeddings ($d{=}32$, gradients enabled) and $H{=}15$, differing only in whether the world model sees $\dt{>}1$ transitions during training, confirming that multi-rate data exposure, not embedding architecture, is the operative variable.

\paragraph{Evidence B: trainability is necessary (exp\_015b).}
Exp\_015b uses a frozen random-projection embedding ($d{=}32$, \texttt{requires\_grad=False}, subsample ON, $H{=}8$), the same multi-rate data regime as exp\_008d, but with a non-trainable temporal signal. Despite full exposure to $\dt{>}1$ transitions, the NVE peaks at $1.45\times$ at 55K but does not sustain escalation: it retreats to $0.65\times$ by 65K and settles at $0.55\times$ by 105K (trajectory: $0.81 \rightarrow 1.26 \rightarrow 1.45 \rightarrow 0.65 \rightarrow 1.05 \rightarrow 0.60 \rightarrow 0.59 \rightarrow 0.55$). The transient peak approaches but does not exceed the $1.5\times$ CIVO threshold, and critically, the ratio self-corrects rather than exhibiting the monotonic escalation seen in trained-embedding runs. Final evaluation reaches only 204.2 at 105K, low but through a qualitatively different failure mode than CIVO. Actor loss approaches zero (gradient starvation; see \S\ref{sec:pathways}), indicating that the frozen embedding provides no useful gradient signal for temporal adaptation.

The contrast with exp\_008d is stark: under identical data conditions (subsample ON, $H{=}8$), the trained embedding drives NVE to $4.12\times$ with sustained monotonic escalation ($1.78 \rightarrow 1.71 \rightarrow 2.09 \rightarrow 4.12$) and triggers full collapse (eval 82.8), while the frozen embedding peaks transiently at $1.45\times$ before self-correcting to $0.55\times$, a qualitative difference between sustained and transient overestimation. This demonstrates that a $\dt$-embedding must be able to \emph{co-adapt} with RSSM dynamics to produce the systematic reward-prediction bias that drives CIVO; a static random projection, regardless of its exposure to multi-rate data, cannot create this bias.

Together, these two controls establish that CIVO requires the \emph{joint} presence of (i)~a trainable embedding that can co-adapt with RSSM dynamics, and (ii)~multi-rate training data that drives this co-adaptation toward biased temporal predictions. Neither factor alone is sufficient.

Cross-environment validation on Cheetah Run replicates the causal pattern: trained $e_{\dt}$ triggers CIVO in both tested seeds (exp\_010b, 2 seeds, NVE $8.27$--$70.64\times$, collapse to 51.7/0.83) while removing the embedding eliminates it (exp\_024b, 2 seeds, NVE $<0.2\times$, eval 96.5/77.3). This extends the necessary-condition finding from Walker Walk to a second locomotion environment with qualitatively different dynamics (6-joint vs.\ 2-joint).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Causal isolation: $e_{\dt}$ configuration $\times$ multi-rate data exposure. The combination of trained embedding and multi-rate exposure is the sole trigger for CIVO. Neither factor alone is sufficient.}
\label{tab:causal_matrix}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
& Multi-rate data (\textsc{on}) & Single-rate data (\textsc{off}) \\
\midrule
Trained $e_{\dt}$ & exp\_008d: \textbf{CIVO} & exp\_008a: healthy \\
 & NVE 4.12$\times$, collapse & NVE 0.93$\times$ \\
\midrule
Frozen $e_{\dt}$ & exp\_015b: grad.\ starvation & --- \\
 & peak NVE 1.45$\times$ (transient) & \\
\midrule
Removed $e_{\dt}$ & exp\_008f: no CIVO & --- \\
 & NVE $\leq$0.97$\times$ & \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Only the upper-left cell (trained embedding with multi-rate exposure) triggers CIVO (Table~\ref{tab:causal_matrix}). This 2$\times$2 structure establishes that both factors are jointly necessary.

\subsection{F4: Degradation Scales Monotonically with \texorpdfstring{$\dt$}{dt}}
\label{sec:f4}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig5_dt_amplification.pdf}
\caption{Peak evaluation return as a function of imagination $\dt$. Performance degrades monotonically: $\dt{=}1$ (915.7) $>$ $\dt{=}2$ (485.3, $-47\%$) $>$ $\dt{=}4$ (176.9, $-81\%$).}
\label{fig:dt_amplification}
\end{figure}

The magnitude of degradation scales monotonically with the imagination step size (Figure~\ref{fig:dt_amplification}). From the $\dt{=}1$ baseline (915.7 at 105K), fixed $\dt{=}2$ (exp\_006) achieves a peak of only 485.3 ($-47\%$), and fixed $\dt{=}4$ (exp\_007) peaks at 176.9 ($-81\%$). This monotonic relationship is consistent with compounding model error: larger temporal gaps amplify the RSSM's prediction inaccuracy at each imagination step, and these errors accumulate across the rollout horizon.

\subsection{F5: Imagination Horizon and Subsampling Are Secondary Factors}
\label{sec:f5}

Neither horizon length nor temporal subsampling triggers collapse without multi-rate data exposure: both trained-but-unexposed configurations (008a, 008c) maintain $\nve < 0.93\times$ (\S\ref{sec:f3}), while the trained-and-exposed counterpart (008d) collapses with $\nve = 4.12\times$.  The no-embedding configurations show cross-seed variance (008f: 503.0 vs.\ 009c: 148.3) but neither exhibits $\nve > 1.5\times$, confirming that low evaluation alone does not indicate CIVO.

The $3.2\times$ performance gap (008a: 777.7 vs.\ 008c: 240.9) quantifies the horizon effect independently of CIVO, as both conditions maintain healthy NVE ($\leq$0.93$\times$).

