\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} shows that conditioning a world model on step size $\dt$ yields consistent gains within TD-MPC2's MPC framework \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2}. Can the same $\dt$-conditioning bring temporal abstraction to imagination-based actor-critic methods like DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, where policies are trained entirely in imagined rollouts?

We find that it cannot. Every variable-rate configuration we test degrades performance catastrophically: fixed $\dt{=}2$ collapses to $14\%$ of baseline, fixed $\dt{=}4$ never exceeds $19\%$ of baseline, and a learned Gumbel-Softmax gate collapses to $11\%$. Through a factorial analysis on Walker Walk \citep{tassa2018dmcontrol}, we isolate the combination of a \emph{trained} $\dt$-embedding and multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor within the factorial design tested. This trained-and-exposed embedding enables the RSSM reward head to develop a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$, which the critic internalizes as optimistic value estimates, a failure mode we term \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO). A $\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold (35K+ window) achieves 13/14 CIVO classification ($n{=}14$, two environments), with one non-CIVO false positive, a failure mode analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} that arises from model imprecision amplified by learned temporal conditioning. Gradient decomposition reveals a non-obvious mechanism: the reward head contributes the largest gradient share (38--49\%) but does not encode $\dt$-specific structural information, while the observation decoder channel (16--22\%) carries the critical spatial predictions that drive the RSSM fidelity gap. Reward-only gradient stopping fails (NVE $10.42\times$) despite targeting the largest pathway; full-pathway SGS that removes observation decoder gradients from the temporal embedding \sgsclaim{} (eval \sgseval{} vs.\ collapse at 40.8). This intervention is necessary but not sufficient, as the 50\% recovery rate exposes additional mechanisms beyond gradient-mediated co-adaptation.

Expanded seed analysis ($n{=}6$, across two environments) confirms that the CIVO trigger universally degrades performance to below 30\% of baseline, with 5/6 seeds progressing toward full collapse at variable onset timing and 1/6 self-correcting to a stable but degraded state. The pathology extends beyond the variable-rate imagination step: restricting imagination to $\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding does not prevent collapse, with two of four seeds collapsing (50\%) via two qualitatively distinct pathways, one CIVO-driven with entropy decrease and one entropy-driven with entropy increase. The bimodal outcome, with no intermediate states, confirms that the trained embedding creates vulnerability through multiple independent mechanisms.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item We identify \emph{conditioning-induced value overestimation} (CIVO) as a failure mode specific to imagination-based world-model RL with temporal abstraction. Through factorial experiments across two environments (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run) with 6 seeds, we isolate trained temporal embedding combined with multi-rate data exposure as the causal trigger (6/6 seeds exceed NVE threshold, 5/6 collapse).
    \item We decompose the CIVO gradient pathway and reveal that gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance: the reward head contributes the largest gradient (38--49\%) yet reward-only gradient stopping fails, while the observation decoder channel (16--22\%) carries the critical fidelity information.
    \item We provide a lightweight online detector that monitors value-to-evaluation ratio dynamics, achieving 100\% sensitivity (5/5 CIVO cases) with predictive warning before performance degradation in 2/5 cases, without requiring a reference baseline.
    \item We identify the feedback architecture (imagination-based bootstrapping versus MPC re-planning) as the structural factor that determines whether $\dt$-conditioning is beneficial or pathological, explaining the contrast with successful dt-conditioning in TD-MPC2.
\end{itemize}

\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:methodology}

\subsection{Base Architecture}
\label{sec:base_arch}

All experiments build on DreamerV3 \citep{hafner2023dreamerv3}, which learns a recurrent state-space model (RSSM) with discrete latent states ($32$ categories $\times$ $32$ classes), uses symlog-transformed predictions, and trains an actor-critic entirely within imagined trajectories. The standard configuration imagines $H{=}15$ steps at a fixed temporal resolution of $\dt{=}1$, meaning each imagination step corresponds to one environment transition.

\subsection{Variable-Rate Imagination Mechanism}
\label{sec:variable_rate}

We introduce three orthogonal architectural modifications that enable variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3. Each can be independently toggled to isolate its contribution.

\paragraph{Temporal embedding.}
A learned embedding $e(\dt)$ is concatenated to the RSSM input at each imagination step to condition state predictions on the temporal scale. We test three modes:
\begin{itemize}[nosep,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Trained}: embedding parameters are updated via backpropagation through the world model loss, so the RSSM can co-adapt its dynamics and reward predictions to the temporal scale.
\item \textbf{Frozen}: a fixed random projection of identical dimensionality (\texttt{requires\_grad=False}). The RSSM receives a temporal signal but cannot co-adapt to it.
\item \textbf{Removed}: embedding dimension set to zero; no temporal conditioning signal is provided.
\end{itemize}
We further distinguish \textbf{trained-and-exposed} (subsample ON: the RSSM trains on $\dt{>}1$ transitions) from \textbf{trained-but-unexposed} (subsample OFF: $\dt{>}1$ used only during imagination). This four-way classification (trained/exposed, trained/unexposed, frozen, removed) is the key causal structure of our factorial design.

\paragraph{Temporal subsampling.}
When enabled, the replay buffer augments training data with variable-$\dt$ transitions by subsampling consecutive frames. Concretely, with a maximum subsampling factor of~2, the RSSM trains on both $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ transitions drawn from the same episodes. When disabled, only standard $\dt{=}1$ transitions are used. This factor controls whether the world model has seen multi-scale dynamics during training.

\paragraph{Imagination horizon and step size.}
The imagination horizon $H$ sets the number of rollout steps. We test $H{=}15$ (default) and $H{=}8$. The reduced horizon controls for compounding error: $H{=}8$ steps at $\dt{=}2$ covers $16$ physical time steps, comparable to the $15$ physical steps of the default configuration. The parameter \texttt{gate\_fixed\_dt} forces all imagination steps to use a specific $\dt$ value ($1$, $2$, or $4$); this separates the effect of $\dt$-conditioning from any learned gating policy.

\subsection{Factorial Design}
\label{sec:factorial}

We adopt a structured factorial ablation across four factors: $\dt$-embedding mode (trained / frozen / none), temporal subsampling (on / off), imagination horizon ($H{=}15$ / $H{=}8$), and imagination step size ($\dt{=}1$, $2$, or $4$). Table~\ref{tab:factorial_design} summarizes the experimental configurations.

